---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import DeckFigure from "../../components/DeckFigure.astro";

const metrics = [
  { value: "63%", label: "Decline in quarterly conversion" },
  { value: "€4.7M", label: "Year-1 net benefit" },
  { value: "46.9%", label: "Year-1 ROI" },
  { value: "7.4x", label: "LTV to CAC, Year 1" },
];

const links = [
  { label: "Deck (PDF)", href: "/media/Linh_Le_Compass_AI_Spotify_Conversion.pdf" },
  { label: "Certificate (PDF)", href: "/media/Linh_Le_ThinkAThon_Runner_Up_Certificate.pdf" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="The best next action is sometimes to say nothing at all"
  description="Spotify's free-to-paid conversion fell 63% while its margins improved. Compass AI, a behavioural decision layer that scores silence as a candidate action. Case study by Linh (Min) Le."
  project="Compass AI, Spotify"
  period="August 2026"
  tint="finance"
  hook="Spotify repaired its income statement by starving the engine that fills it, and the repair looked like a turnaround right up until you separated the two things it was doing at once."
  metrics={metrics}
  links={links}
>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The decision this informed</h2>
        <p>
          Sixty-two percent of Spotify's monthly listeners, some 468 million people, are on the
          free tier. The company has said publicly that it wants a billion users by 2030 with the
          majority of them still free, which rules out the easiest way to push people toward
          Premium, namely making the free experience worse. The question on the table at the Think-A-Thon,
          run by Viet Career Guide at their annual Viet Career Conference, was how to convert more
          of that base without touching it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our team, Wink-N-Win, had to deliver an investment recommendation with a number attached:
          how much to commit, against what return, and under which conditions to stop. I led the
          problem diagnosis and the quantitative work behind it, which meant the financial model and
          the measurement design that would have to hold up if anyone actually spent the money.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other half of the job was translation. Behavioural science, product design and
          financial modelling each turned up with their own vocabulary and their own idea of what
          counted as evidence. Left to themselves the three would have produced a psychology
          argument, a feature concept and a spreadsheet that happened to share a deck.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of that work was unglamorous. The behavioural claim at the centre of the case is that
          somebody hitting the skip limit while putting a playlist together five minutes before
          friends arrive is in a different state from the same person scrolling on a Tuesday
          morning. That only becomes an investment case once someone decides what share of the base
          is ever in that state, how often you can actually reach them there, and how far their
          decision moves when you do. Those three questions became the 40% persuadable share, the
          90% delivery reach and the 2.0 point conditional lift, and the moment they were parameters
          they could be argued with, stress-tested and priced. Every figure quoted anywhere in the
          deck resolves back to one assumptions tab, which is the thing that kept three sections
          from drifting into three versions of the same story.
        </p>
        <p>
          It had to run in the other direction as well. Splitting the conversion decline into
          acquisition and cancellation turned a retention conversation into a targeting one, and a
          targeting problem is won by deciding who to leave alone, which became the sentence the
          product design was built on. Choosing net conversion flow over subscribers divided by
          monthly actives came out of the same instinct, since one is a lever a manager can pull and
          the other is an outcome that moves on its own. Then every behavioural assumption had to be
          handed the number that would prove it wrong: the lift can fall to 1.33 points, the
          persuadable share to 26.6%, reach to 59.8%, and below any of those the recommendation
          stops clearing. Writing them down in advance was how the soft half of the argument was
          made answerable to the hard half. We finished runner-up.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The finding</h2>
        <p>
          Between 2019 and 2026 the rate at which free listeners became paying subscribers fell from
          <strong>4.42% per quarter to 1.64%</strong>, a <strong>63% decline</strong> that a simple
          time regression explains with an R&sup2; of 0.78 at t = &minus;8.18. That is a structural
          drift rather than a noisy quarter, and it ran underneath one of the most celebrated margin
          turnarounds in the company's history: operating margin moved from
          <strong>&minus;5.1% to +15.8%</strong> across the same window, a 20.9 point swing.
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/figures/compass-conversion-decline.png"
          alt="Time series chart of Spotify's quarterly free-to-paid conversion rate, declining steadily across the period covered by the analysis."
          caption="Quarterly conversion flow across the panel. The decline is steady enough that time alone explains 78% of the movement."
        />
        <p>
          The two facts are connected, and the connection runs through sales and marketing. Marketing
          spend fell from <strong>12.0% to 7.5% of revenue</strong> while the new-signup rate fell
          from 15.1% to 7.5% per quarter, the two moving together at a correlation of 0.69. Roughly
          <strong>63% of the margin improvement</strong> came from a one-time cost reset, which is a
          lever that works exactly once.
        </p>
        <p>
          The instinct in a room looking at falling conversion is to blame churn. Splitting the
          decline apart showed cancellations improving over the period, from 9.4% down to 7.3% of the
          paid base. Strip that cushion away and the acquisition weakness is worse than the headline
          suggests, at <strong>&minus;7.65 points against a &minus;5.16 point net decline</strong>.
          Every part of the deterioration traces to people no longer starting to pay.
        </p>
        <p>
          That reframes the entire investment case. A retention problem is solved with offers and
          win-back campaigns. An acquisition problem inside a base of 468 million people is a
          targeting problem, and targeting problems are won by deciding who to leave alone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">What I did, and the obvious alternative I rejected</h2>
        <p>
          The first rejected alternative was a metric, and catching it changed every number
          downstream. The intuitive way to measure free-to-paid conversion is subscribers divided by
          monthly active users, and an early pass of this analysis used exactly that. It is wrong in
          a specific and dangerous way, because it falls whenever the free base grows regardless of
          how the funnel is performing, which makes healthy top-of-funnel growth look like decline.
          Spotify's own reporting already carried a computed conversion rate, on a row the first pass
          had skipped. The analysis was rebuilt on the source definition, and every sheet read now
          resolves by row label instead of row position so the same mistake cannot recur silently.
        </p>
        <div class="pull">
          The published figures come from net conversion flow, meaning net new paying subscribers in
          a quarter over the free base that existed at the end of the previous one. That is a lever
          management can pull. Subscribers over monthly actives is an outcome that moves on its own.
        </div>
        <p>
          The second rejected alternative was the set of levers everyone reaches for first. Cutting
          price erodes the 34.8% Premium gross margin that the 2030 plan depends on, against roughly
          13% on the ad-supported side. Adding features is expensive, copyable, and subject to
          diminishing returns as users adapt. Degrading the free tier reduces sign-ups and works
          directly against the company's own stated free-majority target. Each of those moves the
          product or the price for everyone, which is the wrong shape of instrument for a problem
          where users differ from one another.
        </p>
        <p>
          What survived was a decision layer rather than an offer. Compass AI sorts the eligible base
          by whether an intervention would actually change the outcome, which produces four groups
          and only one worth spending on. Users who would upgrade anyway cost money to convince.
          Users who will never upgrade cost money to annoy. Users already dismissing promotions get
          made worse by another one. The engine acts only on people whose decision it can move, and
          it treats staying silent as a fifth candidate action that every other option has to beat on
          expected value after costs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Building the evidence meant a 32-quarter financial database assembled directly from
          Spotify's SEC filings, covering Q2 2018 through Q1 2026, with 64 dated events mapped to the
          quarter each one landed in. An event-study model then tested whether price changes, feature
          launches or acquisitions explained the conversion decline. None of them did to any
          statistically meaningful degree, which is what moved the diagnosis from product timeline to
          marketing spend.
        </p>
        <p>
          The financial model runs off a single assumptions tab, and every figure quoted anywhere in
          the deck reconciles to it. Base case is a 40% persuadable share of the eligible pool, 90%
          in-app delivery reach, and a 2.0 point conditional lift, producing about 758,000 incremental
          conversions worth <strong>€14.76M</strong> in gross profit against <strong>€10.04M</strong>
          of first-year cost. That leaves <strong>€4.7M</strong> of net benefit at
          <strong>46.9%</strong> return, a <strong>6.7 month</strong> payback on the €6.0M build, and
          a three-year net present value of <strong>€16.34M</strong> discounted at 10%, which is a
          deliberate hurdle rate set well above Spotify's actual cost of capital so the number errs
          low.
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/figures/compass-business-impact-range.png"
          alt="Chart showing the range of modelled Year-1 business impact for Compass AI across scenarios."
          caption="Year-1 impact across the modelled range. The base case sits inside a band rather than at a point, because three of the inputs are assumptions awaiting a pilot."
        />
        <p>
          The part I would defend hardest is the stopping rule. Each load-bearing assumption carries a
          published break-even and a cushion: the lift can fall to 1.33 points, the persuadable share
          to 26.6%, and reach to 59.8% before the case stops clearing, each roughly a 1.5x margin.
          Six assumptions carry the business case in total, and each one is listed with the evidence
          that would replace it, so the pilot is designed to turn modelled inputs into observed ones
          before scale capital is released. Writing that down before the pilot runs is what separates
          an investment case from a pitch.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">Who it acts on</h2>
        <p>
          Targeting runs as two screens rather than one, because the two questions underneath it are
          different questions. The first asks who is worth evaluating at all, on engagement against
          friction. High engagement with repeated friction is the prime target and the only quadrant
          that goes forward. High engagement without friction is a satisfied free user, and the
          instruction there is to protect the experience rather than sell into it. Everything at low
          engagement falls out of scope, because a person who barely uses the product has not yet
          formed the need Premium answers.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second screen is the one that decides spend, and it asks whether the intervention would
          change the outcome at all. Four groups come out of it and only one is worth acting on. The
          persuadable would be more likely to upgrade because of the intervention, so the engine acts.
          The sure thing would have upgraded anyway, so paying to convince them buys nothing. The lost
          cause would still decline, so the spend is wasted. The sleeping dog is the group that makes
          this worth doing carefully, because contacting them makes the experience worse than leaving
          them alone. Three of the four resolve to some version of silence, which is why silence had
          to be a decision the system could take on purpose.
        </p>
        <DeckFigure
          kind="segmentation"
          caption="The two screens, redrawn from the deck. Engagement against friction finds the candidate pool, then persuadability decides who is worth acting on."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">How the decision gets made</h2>
        <p>
          The engine runs in five stages. It observes, building user state from listening behaviour,
          engagement patterns, friction events, offer history and subscription context. It detects a
          relevant moment, which is a specific event rather than a mood: the skip limit reached,
          repeated skips, a high-intent session, a dismissed offer. It generates the set of actions
          that are eligible right then, and staying silent is a member of that set rather than the
          fallback when nothing fires. It evaluates every candidate on persuadability, timing,
          relevance and salience, resolving each to an expected incremental value net of cost. Then it
          acts only when the best available action beats silence, and suppresses when it does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two details make it implementable rather than aspirational. The scoring happens nightly in
          batch, so the live session only has to recognise the trigger, run the eligibility check and
          serve a decision that already exists, which is what keeps it inside a real latency budget.
          And the loop closes: subscribe, dismiss, ignore, churn or retain all feed back into the user
          state that the next cycle observes, so the system is corrected by what actually happened
          rather than by what the model expected.
        </p>
        <DeckFigure
          kind="decision"
          caption="The decision path, redrawn from the deck appendix. Scoring happens nightly, the session detects the trigger and serves a precomputed action, and outcomes return to the state the next cycle reads."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">What we actually asked for, and what would stop it</h2>
        <p>
          The recommendation was deliberately smaller than the build. Spotify was asked to approve
          &euro;4.5M, not the &euro;6.0M the full programme costs, released in three tranches against
          three gates. The first quarter spends &euro;1.5M on the data pipeline and feature store and
          has to prove only that the thing can be measured, with reliable friction triggers, verified
          exposure logging and a valid randomised holdout. The second spends &euro;2.0M on the uplift
          model and has to show an early control-adjusted causal lift in a small live test. The third
          spends &euro;1.0M on a powered multi-market randomised pilot. The final &euro;1.5M rollout
          tranche is not authorised at all until the third gate clears, and running it at scale costs
          about &euro;4.0M a year after that.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same discipline applies after the money is committed. Six triggers were fixed in
          advance, each carrying the action it forces. Measured lift below the 1.33 point
          break-even for two consecutive quarters pauses spend and re-validates against the
          holdout. A persuadable share below 33% at pilot end pauses global rollout and re-tunes
          the uplift model. In-app delivery below 80% for two consecutive months re-opens the reach
          sensitivity and re-baselines. A nudged-versus-control gap in engagement or ad revenue per
          user worse than &minus;1% throttles nudge frequency and remeasures. Nudged-cohort churn
          above 2.0% a month tightens targeting and offers, with LTV to CAC falling to 6.3x.
          Inference cost above its own break-even sends the team back to cloud pricing and batch
          cadence.
        </p>
        <p>
          Writing the exit conditions down before the first tranche is released is what stops a
          programme from being defended after it has stopped working, which is the failure mode
          this kind of initiative actually dies of.
        </p>
        <p>
          The third gate is the one that matters, and it is deliberately joint. Observed lift,
          persuadable share and delivery reach have to support positive forward economics together,
          which stops a strong result on one input covering for a weak result on another. Underneath
          it sits a register of five ways the case could fail, each with the measurement that would
          catch it and the action that follows. If measured lift against the holdout comes in under
          1.33 points, the instruction is to not scale. If the persuadable share falls below 33% or
          delivery reach below 80%, retarget and fix delivery. If free-tier listening or ad revenue
          drops more than 1% against control, throttle, because the whole case depends on not
          damaging the base it draws from. If the cohort the engine converted churns faster than 2.0%
          a month, the offer or the targeting is wrong. And nothing ships on sensitive attributes,
          with policy and eligibility checks clearing before any action is served.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <header class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
        <h2 class="h-sub">The submitted deck</h2>
        <p class="section-note">
          Twenty-three pages, including the appendix carrying the conversion build, the unit
          economics, the break-even sensitivities and the experiment design.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="embed">
        <iframe src="/media/Linh_Le_Compass_AI_Spotify_Conversion.pdf"
                title="Compass AI submitted deck" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
      <p class="embed-note">
        <a href="/media/Linh_Le_Compass_AI_Spotify_Conversion.pdf">Open the deck in its own tab</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
        <p>
          Compass AI was designed and modelled. It was never built and never deployed, so every
          financial figure on this page is a projection under stated assumptions. The measured
          outcome is the second-place finish.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three numbers carry the case and all three are assumptions awaiting a pilot. The 40%
          persuadable share, the 90% delivery reach and the 2.0 point conditional lift were chosen as
          conservative mid-cases and calibrated against published benchmarks, with self-serve freemium
          free-to-paid conversion running 3 to 5 percent overall and mature personalisation programmes
          showing double-digit lifts against low-maturity peers. They remain the model's most
          sensitive inputs.
        </p>
        <p>
          The segmentation rests on a simplifying step that the deck labels as such. Treating the
          high-engagement, high-friction quadrant as 25% of the free base assumes an even distribution
          across a two-by-two screen, and it is a modelling convenience rather than an observed
          Spotify statistic. A pilot should replace it with the real joint distribution.
        </p>
        <p>
          The cancellation rate is anchored to Spotify's own disclosed figure of roughly 4% monthly
          from 2020, which sits inside the range the data cannot rule out. That anchoring pins down
          the shape of the new-signup decline with confidence while leaving the cancellation level
          itself an informed estimate.
        </p>
        <p>
          The link between marketing spend and the signup decline is a correlation of 0.69 across the
          panel. It is the simplest available explanation for a decline the event model cannot account
          for, and it is not proof of causation. Establishing that would need a country-level
          breakdown, which public filings do not support.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
